:title("Go (subset) to LLVM IR compiler") ;
:description("Compiles a Go subset to an LLVM IR module and runs it with the built-in
IR interpreter and the MetaJS handle runtime (llvm.RunJS).

The subset: package and import clauses (parsed, ignored), functions with typed
parameters and multiple return values (func divmod(a int, b int) (int, int)), methods
with value receivers (func (p Point) manhattan() int), struct types with positional
composite literals (Point{3, 4}), field access and assignment, := short declarations
(also multiple: q, r := divmod(7, 2)), var declarations with zero values, parallel
assignment (a, b = b, a) and the blank identifier _, slices ([]int{...}, append, len,
indexing, element assignment), maps (map[K]V{...} literals and make(map[K]V); reads
of missing keys yield the zero value of the value type - int/string/bool -, m[k] = v,
len, delete(m, k), the comma ok idiom v, ok := m[k], and range over the entries),
function literals as values (func(x int) int { ... }) with real closures over their
scope, defer for plain and pkg.fn calls (arguments evaluate at defer time, the calls
run LIFO at function exit), the three for forms (classic, condition only, and
for i, v := range s - also over an int like range 5, Go 1.22, and over maps), switch
with subject
(case 2, 3: compares ==) and without (the first true case wins; no fallthrough, an
implicit break ends every case), Go style if/else without parentheses (else if works), break,
continue, i++ and i-- as statements, assignment operators += -= *= /= %=, fmt.Println
and fmt.Print, os.Exit, and string concatenation. int arithmetic is 32 bit, / truncates.

Widened surface (parsed so a real Go file gets through; -warn-unsupported downgrades
the abort to a warning and keeps the rest compiling, enough for call graphs/CFGs/traces):
a single-result type assertion x.(T) is GENUINELY handled as the identity on the value
(the target type is parsed and ignored), the TYPE SWITCH (switch v := x.(type)) is
genuinely implemented (each arm is a runtime type test), and channel/interface/chan
types are parsed in type positions. Accepted but reported not implemented (no
concurrency, channel, or interface model): interface type declarations, goroutines
(go f()), channel make (make(chan T)), channel send (ch <- v) and receive (<-ch), and
select. Where operands exist (the goroutine callee, the send/receive channel
and value) they still emit under -warn-unsupported so their calls stay visible.

Also implemented (added while widening toward the full language, and measured by
tests/go-test-full.go): grouped declarations - several names sharing a type in
parameters and in struct fields -, multi-name var declarations with a shared type or
an unpacked multi-value initializer, and the full operator set (bitwise and or xor
and-not, shifts, the matching compound assignments, unary complement). A line break
now ends a var spec or a struct field, so a following name is not read as the
previous one's type. Slice expressions (s[lo:hi], byte ranges on a string), the
bracketed conversions []byte(s) / []rune(s), 'for {}', labels with labelled break and
continue and a backward goto, generics ERASED (type parameter lists and instantiations
f[int] / T[K, V] are parsed and ignored), defined types (type myInt int becomes the
conversion to its underlying type), keyed and empty struct literals, Go's own escape
sequences (a byte-wise x and octal escape, and
the eight digit U form), strings as BYTE strings (len and s[i] count bytes, and
ranging one yields byte offsets and runes), the identifier spelled conversions
(string(r), int(x), byte(x), ...), calls on arbitrary values (fs[0]()), variadic
parameters and the f(xs...) spread, named results (a bare return yields them and a
deferred function may still change them), defer on a function literal or a method
value, per iteration loop variables (Go 1.22), an init statement in an if or switch
header, fallthrough, and the min/max/clear builtins.

Deviations and limits: structs behave like references (as if every value were a *T),
append mutates in place (write s = append(s, v) as usual), composite literals must not
appear in if/for/switch headers, map iteration and printing follow the insertion order
(real Go randomizes resp. sorts), 'var m map[K]V' is not a usable map (create maps with
a literal or make), arrays are not copied on assignment and a slice expression COPIES
instead of sharing a backing array, and there are no pointers, method values,
panic/recover, or the comma-ok type assertion.


Functions, methods and function literals become IR functions jsf_N(env, args) (methods
get the receiver
prepended, literals close over their scope via js_closure), struct types become
descriptor objects and composite literals set the fields
positionally (the field lists are known at compile time), method calls dispatch through
js_mcall, multiple return values travel as arrays and are unpacked by multi assignments.
Maps are js_dict_new handles (two parallel key/value arrays keep the insertion order)
with their zero value stored on the map; indexing goes through the polymorphic
js_map_get/js_pyset externals, range loops through js_range_len/key/val (one emission
covers ints, slices and maps), and defer collects [f, args] pairs on a per function
$defers list that js_rundefers runs LIFO before every return.
The whole program becomes jsmain(env, args), which runs the top level and calls main().
The entry point is func main(); self checking programs end with os.Exit(failures).
The same language with the same semantics is interpreted directly by go-interpreter.abnf.") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
:include("lib/cstyle-comments.abnf") ;
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/dq-strings.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

Program     <~~ buildMain(takeAll()) ~~>
            = PackageClause { Import } { TypeDecl | TypeGroup | ConstGroup | ConstSingle | FuncDecl | TopVar } ;

PackageClause = "package" KwEnd Id ;
Import      = "import" KwEnd ( ImportStr | "(" { ImportStr } ")" ) ;
ImportStr   <~~ resolveImport(up.in.slice(1, up.in.length - 1), up.pos) ~~>
            = '"' :whitespace() { "\x20"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x7e" } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// type Point struct { x int; y int } and type Shape interface { Area() int }.
// The name is pushed first; the body decides between a struct (lowered) and an
// interface (accepted, then reported not implemented - no interface semantics).
TypeDecl    <~~ push(makeTypeDecl(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "type" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ TypeParams ] ( StructBody | IfaceBody | DefinedType ) ;
// type myInt int / type Celsius float64: a defined type over an existing one. The value
// model has no distinct type for it, so the name becomes the CONVERSION to its
// underlying type - which is exactly how a defined type is used in an expression.
DefinedType <~~ push({under: firstType(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type ;
// Struct fields: one or more names sharing a type (x, y int), an optional raw string
// tag, and embedded fields (a bare type name, whose field name is the type).
StructBody  = "struct" KwEnd "{" { FieldDecl } "}" ;
FieldDecl   = NamedField | EmbedField ;
NamedField  = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } SameLine FType [ FieldTag ] [ ";" ] ;
// The field's type TEXT, which gives the field its zero value in a literal that
// leaves it out (Go zero-fills; an absent property would read back as undefined).
FType       <~~ push({fty: firstType(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
// An embedded field's NAME is its type name (w.base09, plus the promoted w.id).
EmbedField  = [ "*" ] [ Id "." ] Id <~~ push(up.in); push({fty: ""}) ~~> [ FieldTag ] [ ";" ] ;
FieldTag    = RawString <~~ pop() ~~> ;
IfaceBody   <~~ push({iface: true}) ~~>
            = "interface" KwEnd SkipBlock ;

// A grouped type declaration (type ( A struct {...}; B int )). The whole body is
// balanced-skipped and reported not implemented: unlike a single struct decl, its
// members are not lowered. Tried after TypeDecl (which needs an Id, not '(').
TypeGroup   <~~ push(notImplStmt("grouped type declaration", up.pos)) ~~>
            = "type" KwEnd SkipGroup [ ";" ] ;

// const Name [Type] = expr is GENUINELY bound as if it were a var (its value is
// reused via the var-decl thunk). A grouped const block binds its names too: `iota`
// is the index of the spec inside the group, and a spec with no '=' REPEATS the last
// expression list seen, re-emitted with the new iota (which is how red/green/blue and
// 1 << (10 * iota) count up).
ConstGroup  <~~ push(makeConstGroup(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "const" KwEnd "(" { ConstSpec } ")" [ ";" ] ;
ConstSpec   <~~ push(makeConstSpec(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ SameLine VType ] [ "=" ExprList ] [ ";" ] ;
ConstSingle <~~ push(makeVarDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "const" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ VType ] [ "=" Expression ] [ ";" ] ;

// func name(params) rets { } and func (r Type) name(params) rets { }
FuncDecl    <~~ push(makeFuncStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "func" KwEnd [ Receiver ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ TypeParams ] ParamList [ RetTypes ] Block ;
// Generics are ERASED: a type parameter list is parsed and ignored, the constraint with
// it, and an instantiation (f[int], T[K, V]) is the identity on the value it indexes.
// The value model is dynamically typed, so an erased generic behaves exactly like the
// instantiated one - only compile-time type CHECKING is lost, which a syntax-driven
// front end does not do anyway.
TypeParams  = "[" TypeParamGrp { "," TypeParamGrp } "]" ;
TypeParamGrp = Id { "," Id } Constraint ;
Constraint  = ConstrTerm { "|" ConstrTerm } ;
ConstrTerm  = [ "~" ] Type ;
// A POINTER receiver is marked: its method sees the caller's struct, while a value
// receiver is handed a copy (js_gomcall / js_gofield read the __ptr$name marker).
Receiver    <~~ push({recv: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ "*" <~~ push({byptr: true}) ~~> ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ TypeArgs ] ")" ;
// Parameters: several names may share a type (a, b int), a name may be omitted
// entirely (func(int), only in type positions), and the last one may be variadic.
// A named group is tried first; on an unnamed list it fails and the bare-type form
// takes over.
ParamList   <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" [ ParamItem { "," ParamItem } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
ParamItem   = NamedParam | AnonParam ;
NamedParam  = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ Variadic ] Type ;
AnonParam   = [ Variadic ] Type ;
// The last parameter may be variadic: it collects the remaining arguments as a slice.
Variadic    <~~ push({vararg: true}) ~~> = "..." ;
// f(xs...) spreads a slice into a variadic parameter instead of passing it as one
// argument.
Spread      <~~ push({spread: true}) ~~> = "..." ;
// Results: a single type, or a parenthesized list that may NAME its results
// (func f(a int) (q, r int)). Named results become zero-valued locals that a bare
// 'return' yields and that a deferred function can still change. NamedAhead decides
// which shape it is BEFORE any name is pushed, so the unnamed list never leaks half a
// name list onto the tag stack.
RetTypes    = NamedRets | Type | "(" [ Type { "," Type } ] ")" ;
NamedRets   <~~ push({rets: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = NamedAhead "(" NamedRetGrp { "," NamedRetGrp } ")" ;
NamedRetGrp = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } Type ;
// A parenthesized list is NAMED exactly when some element at depth 1 is two words
// separated by whitespace ('q, r int' has 'r int'; '(int, string)' has no such pair).
NamedAhead = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var depth = 0
        var cc = 0
        var prevWord = false
        var sawSpace = false
        while (i < 8192) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 0) { break }
            var isWord = (cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { sawSpace = true; i++; continue }
            if (cc === 40) { depth++; prevWord = false; sawSpace = false; i++; continue }
            if (cc === 41) {
                depth--
                if (depth === 0) { break }
                prevWord = false
                sawSpace = false
                i++
                continue
            }
            if (depth === 1 && prevWord && sawSpace && (isWord || cc === 91 || cc === 42)) { return undefined }
            prevWord = isWord
            sawSpace = false
            i++
        }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01unnamed-results", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// A package level var: its declaration thunk becomes a top level statement.
// Grouped form (var ( a int; b = 2 )) is GENUINELY bound one spec at a time.
TopVar      = VarGroup | VarDecl ;

Type        = "*" Type | "[" "]" Type | ArrayTypeT | MapTypeT | FuncTypeT | ChanTypeT | IfaceTypeT
            | StructTypeT | ( "int" KwEnd | "string" KwEnd | "bool" KwEnd | Id ) [ "." Id ] [ TypeArgs ] ;
// An anonymous struct type in a TYPE position (var x struct{ a int }): parsed, its
// body balanced-skipped. Tried before the bare-name form, which would otherwise read
// 'struct' as an identifier and stop there.
StructTypeT = "struct" KwEnd SkipBlock ;
TypeArgs    = "[" Type { "," Type } "]" ;
// A pointer type (*T) and a fixed size array ([N]T / [...]T) are parsed in every type
// position; the pointer is transparent (structs already behave like references) and an
// array declares like a slice. The size expression is parsed and ignored.
ArrayTypeT  = "[" ( "..." | Expression <~~ pop() ~~> ) "]" Type ;
MapTypeT    = "map" KwEnd "[" Type "]" Type ;
FuncTypeT   = "func" KwEnd "(" [ Type { "," Type } ] ")" [ Type | "(" Type { "," Type } ")" ] ;
// Channel and (anonymous) interface types are parsed so declarations mentioning
// them type-check syntactically; their values are only produced/consumed through
// constructs that are themselves reported as not implemented.
ChanTypeT   = ( "chan" KwEnd [ "<-" ] | "<-" "chan" KwEnd ) Type ;
IfaceTypeT  = "interface" KwEnd SkipBlock ;

// Every statement production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> undefined | signal.
Statement   <~~ push(stmtPos(pop())) ~~>
            = Block | If | For | Switch | Return | Break | Continue | Goto | Defer | GoStmt | Select | Fallthrough
            | ConstGroup | ConstSingle | VarGroup | VarDecl | Labeled | SimpleStmt | Empty ;

// fallthrough: accepted, reported not implemented (our switch has no fallthrough model).
// fallthrough: continues into the NEXT case body instead of leaving the switch.
Fallthrough <~~ push({isFallthrough: true}) ~~>
            = "fallthrough" KwEnd [ ";" ] ;

// go f(args) / go pkg.Fn(args): a goroutine. Concurrency is COOPERATIVE and
// deterministic (the model of go-interpreter.abnf, shared through the runtime): the
// call is emitted into a closure that js_gospawn only QUEUES, and a receive that
// finds its channel empty runs the queue until one of them fills it.
GoStmt      <~~ push(makeGoStmt(pop(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "go" KwEnd PostfixExpr [ ";" ] ;
// select { case v := <-ch: ...; case ch <- x: ...; default: ... }: the arms are tried
// in source order and the first READY one runs. An arm body is a statement list, so
// NotSelLabel stops it before the next 'case' / 'default' (both would otherwise parse
// as an identifier expression).
Select      <~~ push(makeSelect(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "select" KwEnd "{" { SelArm } "}" ;
SelArm      = SelRecv | SelSend | SelDefault ;
SelRecv     <~~ push(makeSelRecv(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "case" KwEnd [ Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] ( ":=" | "=" ) ]
              "<-" Expression ":" SelBody ;
SelSend     <~~ push(makeSelSend(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "case" KwEnd PostfixExpr "<-" Expression ":" SelBody ;
SelDefault  <~~ push(makeSelDefault(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "default" KwEnd ":" SelBody ;
SelBody     <~~ push(makeSeq(takeAll())) ~~>
            = { SelStmt } ;
SelStmt     = NotSelLabel Statement ;
NotSelLabel = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var cc = 0
        // Whitespace is not skipped before a guard, so step over it (and over line
        // comments) before looking at the keyword.
        while (i < 8192) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01end-of-file", 0) }
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i++; continue }
            if (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) {
                while (i < 8192 && c.peek(i) >= 0 && c.peek(i) !== 10) { i++ }
                continue
            }
            break
        }
        var w = ""
        while (i < 8192 && w.length < 8) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 97 || cc > 122) { break }
            w += String.fromCharCode(cc)
            i++
        }
        if (w === "case" || w === "default") { return abnf.newToken("\x01select-arm-label", 0) }
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// defer f(args) / defer pkg.fn(args): the function value and the arguments are
// evaluated now, the call runs when the surrounding function returns (LIFO).
Defer       <~~ push(makeDefer(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "defer" KwEnd DeferTarget "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } [ Spread ] ] ")" [ ";" ] ;
// Whatever the call is made ON: a name, a package member, a method value or a
// function literal. It is evaluated at defer time, together with the arguments.
// It must NOT swallow the call itself, so the chain carries only the non-call
// suffixes and the primary is restricted to the forms a callee can take.
DeferTarget <~~ push({dfn: pop()}) ~~>
            = DeferChain ;
DeferChain  <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = DeferPrim { FieldSfx | IndexSfx } ;
DeferPrim   = "(" Expression ")" | FuncLit | VarRef ;

Block       <~~ push(makeBlockStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" { Statement } "}" ;

// var x int = 5 / var s string -- the zero value depends on the type.
VarDecl     <~~ push(makeVarDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "var" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ SameLine VType ] [ "=" ExprList ] [ ";" ] ;
VType       <~~ push({ty: up.in}) ~~>
            = Type ;

// var ( a int = 1; b = 2 ): each spec (one name) becomes its own var-decl thunk and
// the group runs them in source order. Tried before VarDecl (needs '(' after var).
VarGroup    <~~ push(makeVarGroup(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "var" KwEnd "(" { VarSpec } ")" [ ";" ] ;
VarSpec     <~~ push(makeVarDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ SameLine VType ] [ "=" ExprList ] [ ";" ] ;

// Short declarations, assignments, ++/-- and expression statements. The cores do
// not eat the separator themselves: for headers reuse them between the semicolons.
// MapOk (v, ok := m[k]) must come first: as a ShortDecl it would miss the flag.
SimpleStmt  = ( MapOk | TypeOk | RecvOk | ShortDecl | IncDecStmt | Assign | ChanSend | ExprOnly ) [ ";" ] ;

// ch <- v: a channel send. The left side is a postfix expression (so the leading
// '<' of a relational compare is never mistaken for the '<-' send token: a compare
// is written 'a < -b' with a space). Accepted, then reported not implemented; both
// operands still evaluate under -warn-unsupported so their calls stay visible.
// v, ok := <-ch: the received value and whether the channel was still OPEN. It must
// be tried before ShortDecl, which would destructure the value itself.
RecvOk      <~~ push(makeRecvOk(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
              ( ":=" <~~ push({decl: true}) ~~> | "=" <~~ push({decl: false}) ~~> ) "<-" Expression ;
ChanSend    <~~ push(makeChanSend(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = PostfixExpr "<-" Expression ;

// The comma ok idiom: the value (or the zero value) plus the key presence flag.
MapOk       <~~ push(makeMapOk(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ":=" Primary "[" Expression "]" ;
// The comma ok TYPE ASSERTION: v, ok := x.(T). Without it the pair reads as a plain
// two-name ShortDecl of one value, which destructures the value itself (a string
// assertion then bound its first character). Only the Primary base is covered, the
// same restriction MapOk has.
TypeOk      <~~ push(makeTypeOk(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ":=" Primary "." "(" AssertType ")" ;
AssertType  <~~ push({at: firstType(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
ExprOnly    <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = Expression ;

ShortDecl   <~~ push(makeShortDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } ":=" ExprList ;

IncDecStmt  <~~ push(makeIncDec(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target ( "++" | "--" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ;

// a = e / a, b = b, a / p.x = e / s[i] = e / a += e
Assign      <~~ push(makeAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target { "," Target } AssignOp <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> ExprList ;
// Longest first: '&^=' before '&=', and the bare '=' last of all.
AssignOp    = "&^=" | "<<=" | ">>=" | "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%=" | "&=" | "|=" | "^=" | "=" ;

Target      <~~ push(makeTargetRef(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "." Id <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> | TIndex } ;
TIndex      <~~ push({idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" ;

ExprList    = Expression { "," Expression } ;

Empty       <~~ push(function(b) { return b }) ~~>
            = ";" ;

// Go style headers without parentheses. Composite literals are gated off inside.
If          <~~ push(makeIfG(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "if" KwEnd CondOn [ InitAhead FInit ";" ] Expression CondOff Block [ "else" KwEnd ( If | Block ) ] ;

// ForCond is tried before ForClassic: a classic header would leave the
// composite literal gate pushed when it fails at its first semicolon.
For         = ForEver | ForRange | ForCond | ForClassic ;
// 'for {}': tried first, because the other forms would push the composite-literal
// gate and then fail on the '{', leaking the marker. Each form claims the pending
// label (see Labeled) at the 'for' keyword, BEFORE its body is built, so a nested
// loop cannot steal the outer loop's label.
ForEver     <~~ push(makeForEver(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "for" KwEnd <~~ push({lbl: takeLabel()}) ~~> Block ;
// for i, v := range s / for _, v := range s / for i := range s
ForRange    <~~ push(makeForRange(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "for" KwEnd <~~ push({lbl: takeLabel()}) ~~> Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] ":="
              "range" KwEnd CondOn Expression CondOff Block ;
ForClassic  <~~ push(makeForClassic(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "for" KwEnd <~~ push({lbl: takeLabel()}) ~~> CondOn [ FInit ] ";" [ FCond ] ";" [ FPost ] CondOff Block ;
FInit       <~~ push({k:"init", t:pop(), names: takeDeclNames()}) ~~> = SimpleStmt2 ;
FCond       <~~ push({k:"cond", t:pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
FPost       <~~ push({k:"post", t:pop()}) ~~> = SimpleStmt2 ;
SimpleStmt2 = MapOk | TypeOk | RecvOk | ShortDecl | IncDecStmt | Assign ;
ForCond     <~~ push(makeWhileG(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "for" KwEnd <~~ push({lbl: takeLabel()}) ~~> CondOn Expression CondOff Block ;

// Go switch: no fallthrough (an implicit break ends every case), several values per
// case, with a subject (compared ==) or without one (the first true condition wins).
Switch      = TypeSwitch | ValueSwitch ;
// switch v := x.(type) { case int: ... }: the subject is evaluated once, every case
// lists TYPES (compared with the runtime type test js_gotypeis), and the bound name
// takes the subject's value inside the matching case body. Tried before the value
// switch so its subject's '.(type)' is not mistaken for a value plus a type assertion.
TypeSwitch  <~~ push(makeTypeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "switch" KwEnd TypeSwAhead [ Id <~~ push({tsname: up.in}) ~~> ":=" ]
              CondOn TSSubject CondOff "." "(" "type" KwEnd ")"
              "{" { TypeCase | Statement } "}" ;
TSSubject   <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Primary { MethodSfx | FieldSfx | IndexSfx } ;
TypeCase    <~~ push(makeTypeCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "case" KwEnd CaseType { "," CaseType }
              | "default" KwEnd ) ":" ;
CaseType    <~~ push({ct: up.in}) ~~>
            = Type ;
// The subjectless body is tried before the subject so a bare 'switch {' never enters
// SwitchSubj: that would push the composite-literal gate marker (CondOn) and then fail
// its Expression on the '{', leaking the marker (pushes are not rolled back) and
// blocking every later composite literal.
ValueSwitch <~~ push(makeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "switch" KwEnd [ InitAhead FInit ";" ]
              ( "{" { CaseLabel | Statement } "}"
              | SwitchSubj "{" { CaseLabel | Statement } "}" ) ;
SwitchSubj  <~~ push({subj: pop()}) ~~>
            = CondOn Expression CondOff ;
// The body is parsed flat: the labels must be tried before Statement (a bare 'case'
// would parse as an identifier expression), and the builder groups the statements
// under the label they follow.
CaseLabel   <~~ push(makeCaseLabel(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "case" KwEnd Expression <~~ push({v: pop()}) ~~> { "," Expression <~~ push({v: pop()}) ~~> }
              | "default" KwEnd ) ":" ;

Return      <~~ push(makeReturn(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "return" KwEnd [ Expression { "," Expression } ] [ ";" ] ;

// break / continue carry an optional label: they leave (resp. re-enter) the loop that
// carries that label rather than the innermost one.
Break       <~~ push(makeBreakG(takeAll())) ~~>
